          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Global Circulations and Jet Streams ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 28.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              100.80000305175781,
              75.60729217529297,
              510.68499755859375,
              103.62728881835938
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "9.1",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              117.86051177978516,
              88.2750015258789,
              130.88050842285156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Introduction ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              100.80000305175781,
              117.86051177978516,
              168.3350067138672,
              130.88050842285156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Global circulations explain how air and storm systems, which have potential impacts on aircraft operations, ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              138.60244750976562,
              542.8049926757812,
              149.5824432373047
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "travel over the Earth’s surface. Jet streams are relatively narrow bands of strong wind in the upper levels of ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              151.20242309570312,
              542.6055908203125,
              162.1824188232422
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the atmosphere. This chapter will discuss global circulations and jet streams. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              163.86245727539062,
              412.10498046875,
              174.8424530029297
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 111,
        "elements": []
      },
      {
        "page_number": 112,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p112_b10.png",
            "bbox": [
              90.0,
              76.5,
              521.9400024414062,
              411.8500061035156
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 9-2. Earth Circulation System ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              424.3351135253906,
              222.60501098632812,
              434.3551025390625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Between each of these circulation cells are bands of high and low pressure at the surface. The high-pressure ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              448.8824462890625,
              542.7813720703125,
              459.8624267578125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "band is located about 30° N/S latitude and at each pole. Low-pressure bands are found at the Equator ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              461.4824523925781,
              542.7213745117188,
              472.4624328613281
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "and 50° to 60° N/S latitude. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              474.1424560546875,
              196.625,
              485.1224365234375
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Usually, fair and dry/hot weather is associated with high pressure, and rainy and stormy weather is ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              492.8024597167969,
              542.7725219726562,
              503.7824401855469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "associated with low pressure. The results of these circulations become evident on a globe. Consider ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              505.4624328613281,
              542.722412109375,
              516.4424438476562
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "the number of deserts located along the 30° N/S latitude around the world compared to the region ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              518.1223754882812,
              542.6845092773438,
              529.1024169921875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "between 50°–60° N/S latitude. The higher latitudes, especially near the west coast of continents, tend to ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              530.7824096679688,
              542.4336547851562,
              541.762451171875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "have more precipitation due to more storms moving around the Earth at these latitudes. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              543.4424438476562,
              457.8249816894531,
              554.4224853515625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "9.4",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              568.260498046875,
              88.2750015258789,
              581.2804565429688
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Jet Streams ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              100.80000305175781,
              568.260498046875,
              163.65499877929688,
              581.2804565429688
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Jet streams are relatively narrow bands of strong wind in the upper levels of the atmosphere. The winds ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              588.982421875,
              542.72412109375,
              599.9624633789062
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "blow from west to east in jet streams, but the flow often meanders southward and northward in waves. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              601.6423950195312,
              542.4158325195312,
              612.6224365234375
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Jet streams follow the boundaries between hot and cold air. Since these hot and cold air boundaries are most ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              614.3024291992188,
              542.5877075195312,
              625.282470703125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "pronounced in winter, jet streams are the strongest for both the Northern and Southern Hemisphere winters. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              626.96240234375,
              542.8049926757812,
              637.9424438476562
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "9.4.1",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              651.8405151367188,
              97.98790740966797,
              664.8604736328125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Direction of Wind Flow ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              108.0199966430664,
              651.8405151367188,
              236.5550079345703,
              664.8604736328125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Why do the jet stream winds blow from west to east? As stated in the previous section, if the Earth was not ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              672.5624389648438,
              542.5256958007812,
              683.54248046875
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "rotating, the warm air would rise at the Equator and move toward both the poles. The Earth’s rotation ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              685.242431640625,
              542.7755737304688,
              696.2224731445312
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "divides this circulation into three cells. Likewise, the Earth’s rotation is responsible for the jet stream. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              697.9024047851562,
              521.9049682617188,
              708.8824462890625
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 113,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The motion of the air is not directly north and south, but rather is affected by the momentum the air has as ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              73.42244720458984,
              542.3280639648438,
              84.40245056152344
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "it moves away from the Equator and how fast a location on or above the Earth moves relative to the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              86.0824203491211,
              542.6359252929688,
              97.06242370605469
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Earth’s axis. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              98.7424545288086,
              129.72500610351562,
              109.72245788574219
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "An object’s speed relative to the Earth’s axis depends on its location. Someone standing on the Equator is ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              117.3424301147461,
              542.51123046875,
              128.3224334716797
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "moving much faster than someone standing on a 45-degree latitude line. In Figure 9-3, the person at the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              130.02243041992188,
              542.7162475585938,
              141.00242614746094
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "position on the Equator arrives at the yellow line sooner than the other two. Someone standing on a pole is ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              142.68246459960938,
              542.5366821289062,
              153.66246032714844
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "not moving at all (except that person would be slowly spinning). The speed of the rotation is great enough ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              155.34243774414062,
              542.5243530273438,
              166.3224334716797
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "to cause a person to weigh 1 pound (lb) less at the Equator than they would at the North or South Pole. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              168.00247192382812,
              526.2849731445312,
              178.9824676513672
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p113_b9.png",
            "bbox": [
              198.0,
              195.69000244140625,
              413.97998046875,
              429.3399963378906
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 9-3. Speed Relative to the Earth’s Axis Versus Latitude ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              441.55511474609375,
              325.64501953125,
              451.5751037597656
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The momentum of air as it travels around the Earth is conserved, which means as the air that is over the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              466.0424499511719,
              542.35009765625,
              477.0224304199219
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Equator starts moving toward one of the poles, it keeps its eastward motion constant. The Earth below the ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              478.70245361328125,
              542.3169555664062,
              489.68243408203125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "air, however, moves slower, as that air travels toward the poles. The result is that the air moves faster and ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              491.3624572753906,
              542.547119140625,
              502.3424377441406
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "faster in an easterly direction (relative to the Earth’s surface below) the farther it moves from the Equator. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              504.0224304199219,
              540.885009765625,
              515.00244140625
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "9.4.2",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              528.9005126953125,
              97.98790740966797,
              541.9204711914062
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Location ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 13.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              108.0199966430664,
              528.9005126953125,
              157.47500610351562,
              541.9204711914062
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "In addition, with the three cell circulations mentioned previously, the regions around 30° N/S and  ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              549.6224365234375,
              545.5650024414062,
              560.6024780273438
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "50°–60° N/S are areas where temperature changes are the greatest (see Figure 9-4). As the difference in ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              562.222412109375,
              542.73095703125,
              573.2024536132812
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "temperature between the two locations increases, the strength of the wind increases. Therefore, the regions ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              574.8823852539062,
              542.5368041992188,
              585.8624267578125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "around 30° N/S and 50°–60° N/S are also regions where the wind in the upper atmosphere is the strongest. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              587.5424194335938,
              542.8049926757812,
              598.5224609375
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "page_number": 114,
        "elements": []
      },
      {
        "page_number": 115,
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "image",
            "image_path": "/images/Weather_p115_b6.png",
            "bbox": [
              198.0,
              76.50001525878906,
              413.95001220703125,
              206.95001220703125
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Figure 9-6. Jet Stream Wind Speeds ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.020000457763672,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              218.81512451171875,
              218.885009765625,
              228.8351287841797
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The jet stream is often indicated by a line on maps and is displayed this way by television meteorologists. ",
            "font_name": "TimesNewRomanPSMT",
            "font_size": 10.979999542236328,
            "color": "#000000",
            "is_bold": false,
            "is_italic": false,
            "bbox": [
              72.0,
              243.36245727539062,
              542.6893310546875,
              254.3424530029297
            ]
          },
          {
